    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acral lentiginous melanoma of the right great toe, pathologic stage IA (pT1b pN0(sn) M0), after digit-sparing excision (C43.71)</content>. The excision is complete with clear margins and no residual invasive melanoma, and both sentinel nodes are negative: pathologic stage IA, with an excellent outlook. Adjuvant immunotherapy or targeted therapy is not indicated at stage IA; it is considered from stage IIB. The toe has been kept, as he chose, without compromising the cancer treatment. Surveillance is by history and examination, with no routine scans or blood tests. [S]<list><item>No adjuvant therapy.</item><item>Surveillance: history and examination of the skin, nails, graft and nodes every 6 to 12 months for 5 years, then yearly; a first review at about three months to check the graft; a yearly full skin examination by dermatology.</item><item>Graft check by the surgical team at six weeks; running once the graft has fully healed, and football after that check.</item><item>Monthly skin self-examination taught today.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Final pathology of the excision and sentinel nodes</td><td>After definitive surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No residual invasive melanoma; margins clear, closest 6 mm; 0 of 2 sentinel nodes involved</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>AJCC 8th edition pathologic stage</td><td>After definitive surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>pT1b pN0(sn) M0, stage IA</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adjuvant systemic therapy</td><td>Considered from stage IIB</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Stage IA</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Graft, donor site, and groin wound</td><td>Each visit until healed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Graft 95 percent taken; groin and arm healing; no infection or seroma</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Examination of the lymph node basins (right popliteal and inguinal) and the scar</td><td>At diagnosis, then at each surveillance visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No nodes; groin scar healed</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging (CT, PET, or ultrasound) and LDH</td><td>Not routine for stage I to II without symptoms</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Stage IA without symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Skin self-examination</td><td>Monthly, taught after diagnosis and reviewed each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taught today with a mirror and a handout: the palms, soles, between the toes and all nails, the sites people with darker skin most need to check, and the graft and both groins; he will photograph the graft each month</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Footwear and return to activity</td><td>After nail unit surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Stiff-soled shoe until the graft has healed; running after it heals; football after the six-week graft check</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain score</td><td>Each visit after surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Worst pain 2 of 10, only after walking far</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>At each phase of care</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Tissue diagnosis within two weeks of presentation. Outcome by 2026-06-17: Dermoscopy and nail matrix biopsy two days after presentation; diagnosis explained on the ninth day [S]</item><item>Definitive surgery with clear margins and lymph node staging within four weeks of diagnosis, by the approach he chose. Outcome by 2026-07-15: Digit-sparing excision of the nail unit with clear margins and a negative sentinel node two weeks after diagnosis [S]</item><item>Walk, run, and play football normally within four months of surgery. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Monthly skin and nail self-examination, including the palms, soles, between the toes and all nails. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Melanoma: Cutaneous, Version 3.2026 (2026); Swetter SM, et al. Guidelines of care for the management of primary cutaneous melanoma. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019;80(1):208-250 (2019); Gershenwald JE, et al. Melanoma staging: Evidence-based changes in the American Joint Committee on Cancer eighth edition cancer staging manual. CA Cancer J Clin 2017;67(6):472-492 (2017); Chouquet L, et al. Reconstruction After Wide Excision of the Nail Apparatus in the Treatment of Melanoma: A Systematic Literature Review. J Clin Med 2025;14(17):5932 (2025); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Graft check by the surgical team at six weeks. Preventive visit in about two months. Melanoma review about three months after surgery. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-21: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
